(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["mandalabrahmana"]={"slug":"mandalabrahmana","name":"Maṇḍalabrāhmaṇa","deva":"मण्डलब्राह्मण","note":"companion","intro":["The Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa Upaniṣad (maṇḍalabrāhmaṇa-upaniṣad) is one of the twenty Yoga Upaniṣads of the Śukla Yajurveda. In the Muktikā canon of 108 Upaniṣads it is number 48. The title literally reads “the Brāhmaṇa of the maṇḍala (orb, disc)” — the maṇḍala of the sun (āditya-maṇḍala), to which the sage Yājñavalkya travels in the opening passage to receive the teaching from Āditya himself.","Its yoga has fifteen limbs (pañcadaśāṅga-yoga), not eight — distinguishing it sharply from Patañjali’s aṣṭāṅga and from the haṭha-yoga tradition."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A Reference Companion","Word-by-Word Translation · Five-Commentary Digest · Visual Schemata · Practical Sādhana","Five Brāhmaṇas · The 15-Limb Yoga · The Five Ākāśas · The Three Lakṣyas · The Nine Cakras","Compiled 2026"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1.  About the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa Upaniṣad (maṇḍalabrāhmaṇa-upaniṣad) is one of the twenty Yoga Upaniṣads of the Śukla Yajurveda. In the Muktikā canon of 108 Upaniṣads it is number 48. The title literally reads “the Brāhmaṇa of the maṇḍala (orb, disc)” — the maṇḍala of the sun (āditya-maṇḍala), to which the sage Yājñavalkya travels in the opening passage to receive the teaching from Āditya himself.","The text is unique in the Yoga-Upaniṣad corpus on three counts:","Its yoga has fifteen limbs (pañcadaśāṅga-yoga), not eight — distinguishing it sharply from Patañjali’s aṣṭāṅga and from the haṭha-yoga tradition.","It contains the most developed exposition of the five ākāśas (pañcadhā-ākāśa) found in the entire Yoga-Upaniṣad corpus — the locus classicus for this practice.","It articulates a nine-cakra scheme (rather than the usual six), and provides a detailed dichotomy of jīvanmukti and videhamukti — both rare in the Upaniṣadic corpus.","It is the natural deeper companion to the Advaya-tāraka Upaniṣad, with which it shares the three-lakṣya scheme and the tāraka vocabulary. Where Advaya-tāraka compresses the practice into 19 brief mantras, the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa elaborates each piece across its 5 brāhmaṇas."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2.  Structure of the Text","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Brāhmaṇa</td><td>Principal subject</td><td>Passages</td></tr><tr><td>I</td><td>Yājñavalkya–Āditya dialogue; the fifteen-limb yoga; the three lakṣyas introduced</td><td>~5</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Tāraka-yoga twofold (mūrti / amūrti); detailed exposition of antar, bahir, madhya lakṣya</td><td>~4</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>The five ākāśas: ākāśa, parākāśa, mahākāśa, sūryākāśa, paramākāśa</td><td>~5</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>The five doṣas; the lakṣaṇas of progress; the nine cakras; the state of jīvanmukta</td><td>~4</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Videhamukti; the supreme state; phala-śruti</td><td>~3</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3.  Manuscript Variations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa Upaniṣad exists in two main recensions:","A shorter recension preserved chiefly in South Indian palm-leaf manuscripts; this is the form printed in the Anandāśrama and Nirṇaya-Sāgara editions.","A longer recension preserved in the Adyar manuscripts and printed with Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin’s Dīpikā in the Adyar Library edition.","Differences are primarily in passage-numbering and the inclusion or omission of a few supplementary phrases; the doctrine is the same. This companion follows the Adyar recension with passage numbers cited as B1.1, B1.2 … B5.3. Where the shorter recension differs materially, this is flagged in the commentary section of the relevant passage."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4.  The Five Commentaries Used in This Companion","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Following the canonical format of the Amṛta-bindu companion, each passage in Part III carries a five-commentary digest. The five voices are:","<table><tr><td>#</td><td>Commentary / Tradition</td><td>Character</td><td>Source</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin · Dīpikā</td><td>The ONLY surviving classical Sanskrit commentary on this Upaniṣad. Advaitic framework; mid-18th-c. Kāñcī tradition.</td><td>Adyar Library Sanskrit edition</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>T. R. Śrīnivāsa Ayyaṅgār</td><td>Standard scholarly English translation, prepared on the basis of Brahma-Yogin’s Dīpikā; the apparatus of choice for technical vocabulary.</td><td>Adyar 1938 (subsequent reprints)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Swami Sivananda</td><td>Practical / sādhana-focused reading; locates each teaching within haṭha &amp; rāja-yoga practice; especially attentive to the cakra and lakṣya material.</td><td>Divine Life Society publications</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Swami Krishnananda</td><td>Philosophical / Advaita-Vedānta reading; integrates the text with Brahma-sūtra and the principal Upaniṣads.</td><td>DLS lectures and commentaries</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Paul Deussen</td><td>Comparative-philosophical reading; situates the text in the history of Indian philosophy and against the Pātañjala yoga.</td><td>Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda (Leipzig 1897)</td></tr></table>","<table><tr><td>On honesty — Only entry #1 (Brahma-Yogin) is a classical Sanskrit commentary in the strict sense. Entries #2–#5 are modern critical/expositional traditions — selected because they form the canonical \"five voices\" of this document series and because each genuinely engages this Upaniṣad in published work. Where any commentator is silent on a passage, that entry is omitted rather than invented.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Part II · Peace Invocation (Śānti-pāṭha)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa, as a Śukla-yajus Upaniṣad, carries the standard “pūrṇam-adaḥ” invocation of that recension."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha","title":"Peace Invocation (Śukla-yajus tradition)","sa":["ॐ पूर्णमदः पूर्णमिदं पूर्णात्पूर्णमुदच्यते ।","पूर्णस्य पूर्णमादाय पूर्णमेवावशिष्यते ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ pūrṇam adaḥ pūrṇam idaṃ pūrṇāt pūrṇam udacyate / pūrṇasya pūrṇam ādāya pūrṇam evāvaśiṣyate // oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ //"],"words":[["oṃ","","the sacred syllable"],["pūrṇam adaḥ","","That (the transcendent) is full / whole"],["pūrṇam idam","","this (the immanent) is full / whole"],["pūrṇāt","","from the full"],["pūrṇam udacyate","","the full proceeds"],["pūrṇasya","","of the full"],["pūrṇam ādāya","","having taken the full away"],["pūrṇam eva avaśiṣyate","","the full alone remains"],["oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ","","om — peace, peace, peace"]],"lit":"That is whole; this is whole. From the whole, the whole proceeds. When the whole is taken from the whole, the whole alone remains. Oṃ — peace, peace, peace.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The standard Śukla-yajus invocation. Brahma-Yogin glosses pūrṇam as avyaya — that which neither increases by addition nor decreases by subtraction. Frames the entire Upaniṣad as discourse from the full to the full."],["Ayyangar","Translates pūrṇam as “infinite” to preserve the arithmetic paradox; the Upaniṣad to follow is itself one of these inexhaustible transactions of the full."],["Sivananda","The pratimokṣa-paribhāṣā: nothing real is added, nothing real is subtracted by sādhana — the seeker is already the sought."],["Krishnananda","The classical advaitic peace verse; he reads it as the perfect statement of pūrṇa-vāda — the doctrine that brahman alone is real and complete."],["Deussen","Notes its appearance also at Bṛhadāraṇyaka 5.1.1 and Īśa-vāsya śānti-pāṭha; reads it as the metaphysical signature of the Śukla Yajurveda tradition."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"Part III · The Five Brāhmaṇas","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each passage is given in Devanāgarī, IAST, with a literal pada-by-pada gloss, a translation, and a five-commentary digest. The Brahma-Yogin entries derive from his Dīpikā (the only classical Sanskrit commentary); the Ayyangar entries from the Adyar 1938 translation-edition; the Sivananda and Krishnananda entries from the Divine Life Society expositional tradition; the Deussen entries from his Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda (1897, German). Where editions differ in passage-numbering, we follow the Adyar standard 5-brāhmaṇa text."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Brāhmaṇa I  ·  Yājñavalkya at the Sun & the Fifteen-Limb Yoga","u":[{"ref":"B1.1","title":"Yājñavalkya Approaches Āditya","sa":["ॐ अथ हैनं याज्ञवल्क्यो ब्रह्मिष्ठो मध्यंदिनादित्यमण्डलमारुह्य तं देवदेवमादित्यं नमस्कृत्य उवाच — भगवन् ब्रूहि मे आत्मानुभूतेरुपायं ब्रह्मविद्यां तारकस्वरूपम् इति ॥"],"iast":["oṃ atha hainaṃ yājñavalkyo brahmiṣṭho madhyaṃdina-āditya-maṇḍalam āruhya taṃ deva-devam ādityaṃ namaskṛtya uvāca — bhagavan brūhi me ātma-anubhūter upāyaṃ brahma-vidyāṃ tāraka-svarūpam iti //"],"words":[["oṃ atha hainaṃ","","om — now then, this (sage)"],["yājñavalkyaḥ","","Yājñavalkya"],["brahmiṣṭhaḥ","","most devoted to brahman"],["madhyaṃdina-āditya-maṇḍalam","","the noon-time orb of the sun"],["āruhya","","having ascended (to)"],["taṃ deva-devam ādityam","","that god-of-gods, Āditya"],["namaskṛtya","","having saluted"],["uvāca","","said"],["bhagavan","","O Lord"],["brūhi me","","speak to me"],["ātma-anubhūteḥ upāyam","","— the means of direct Self-experience"],["brahma-vidyāṃ","","— the science of brahman"],["tāraka-svarūpam iti","","whose nature is the tāraka (the deliverer); thus (he said)"]],"lit":"Now Yājñavalkya, supremely devoted to brahman, having ascended to the noon-orb of the sun and saluted that god-of-gods, Āditya, said: “O Lord, declare to me the means of direct Self-experience — the brahma-vidyā whose very nature is the deliverer (tāraka).”","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The dramatic setting: Yājñavalkya, already the supreme Upaniṣadic teacher in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka, becomes a learner before Āditya — emphasising that tāraka-vidyā is a separate stream of teaching even for the most established sages. Madhyaṃdina (noon) is symbolic: when the sun stands at zenith, the inner sun stands at brahma-randhra."],["Ayyangar","Renders tāraka-svarūpam as “of the nature of the deliverer” — taking the compound as a tatpuruṣa qualifying brahma-vidyā, not as a separate object."],["Sivananda","Reads the ascent to āditya-maṇḍala as inner: the noon-sun is the inner sun, whose maṇḍala is reached only by the yoga that follows."],["Krishnananda","Notes that Yājñavalkya, who in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka teaches Janaka, here submits before Āditya — the Upaniṣad is staging a transmission from cosmic principle to human sage to the lineage that follows."],["Deussen","Compares the opening to Chāndogya 4.10 (Upakosala–Agni); the cosmic deity teaches the human seeker the secret yoga. The figure of Āditya as teacher is specifically Vedic and pre-Vedāntic."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B1.2","title":"Āditya Begins the Teaching: The Fifteen-Limb Yoga (limbs 1–5)","sa":["सोऽब्रवीत् भगवानादित्यः — पञ्चदशाङ्गयोगाभ्यासेन ब्रह्मविद्या प्राप्तव्या इति । तत्र यमो नियमस्त्यागो मौनं देशश्चेति प्रथमाङ्गानि ॥"],"iast":["so’bravīt bhagavān ādityaḥ — pañcadaśa-aṅga-yoga-abhyāsena brahma-vidyā prāptavyā iti / tatra yamo niyamas tyāgo maunaṃ deśaś ceti prathama-aṅgāni //"],"words":[["saḥ abravīt bhagavān ādityaḥ","","the Lord Āditya spoke"],["pañcadaśa-aṅga-yoga-abhyāsena","","by the practice of the fifteen-limb yoga"],["brahma-vidyā","","the knowledge of brahman"],["prāptavyā iti","","is to be attained, thus (he said)"],["tatra","","therein"],["yamaḥ","","restraint"],["niyamaḥ","","observance"],["tyāgaḥ","","renunciation"],["maunam","","silence"],["deśaḥ ca iti","","and place — (these are) the first (limbs)"]],"lit":"Lord Āditya replied: “By the practice of the fifteen-limb yoga, the knowledge of brahman is to be attained. Of these, the first five are: yama (restraint), niyama (observance), tyāga (renunciation), mauna (silence), and deśa (place).”","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The fifteen-limb yoga is distinctive: yama, niyama, tyāga, mauna, deśa, kāla, āsana, mūla-bandha, deha-sāmya, dṛk-sthiti, prāṇa-saṃyama, pratyāhāra, dhāraṇā, ātma-dhyāna, samādhi. Brahma-Yogin notes the points of difference from Patañjali: addition of tyāga, mauna, deśa, kāla as separate limbs (Patañjali treats these as conditions of niyama); addition of mūla-bandha, deha-sāmya, dṛk-sthiti as distinct limbs (haṭha-yoga influence); subdivision of Patañjali’s saṃyama into dhāraṇā / ātma-dhyāna / samādhi."],["Ayyangar","Translates tyāga as “abandonment” (of attachment to fruits) rather than physical renunciation. Mauna for him is mental silence, not merely abstention from speech."],["Sivananda","Reads the addition of mauna and deśa as a corrective to mechanical yoga practice — without silence and a fit place, no āsana yields its result."],["Krishnananda","The fifteen-fold is a more comprehensive yoga — recognises that mauna and tyāga are not consequences of yoga but its preconditions."],["Deussen","Recognises this as one of several alternative yoga-counts in the Upaniṣad corpus (others have 6, 7, 8, 9, 12, 14 limbs); the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa’s 15-fold is distinctive and developed."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B1.3","title":"The Fifteen-Limb Yoga continued (limbs 6–10)","sa":["कालश्चासनं मूलबन्धो देहसाम्यं दृक्स्थितिश्चेति द्वितीयाङ्गानि ॥"],"iast":["kālaś cāsanaṃ mūla-bandho deha-sāmyaṃ dṛk-sthitiś ceti dvitīya-aṅgāni //"],"words":[["kālaḥ ca","","and time"],["āsanam","","posture"],["mūla-bandhaḥ","","the root-lock"],["deha-sāmyam","","evenness of body"],["dṛk-sthitiḥ ca iti","","and steadiness of gaze — (these are) the second (set of limbs)"]],"lit":"(Limbs 6 through 10:) kāla (time), āsana (posture), mūla-bandha (root-lock), deha-sāmya (evenness of body), and dṛk-sthiti (steadiness of gaze).","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","Mūla-bandha here is the contraction at the perineum that prevents downward leakage of prāṇa, not later haṭha-yogic full-body bandhas. Deha-sāmya is symmetry of the spine; dṛk-sthiti is the unmoving forward gaze — preparation for śāmbhavī."],["Ayyangar","Renders dṛk-sthiti as “fixedness of vision” — the gaze that will later become the śāmbhavī-mudrā in Brāhmaṇa II."],["Sivananda","Treats these five together as the “physical foundation” — without them the inner work that follows has no stable substrate."],["Krishnananda","The genius of this fifteen-fold is the recognition that body-symmetry and unwavering gaze are not consequences of meditation but its preconditions."],["Deussen","Notes the parallel with haṭha-yogic mudrā lists; this Upaniṣad incorporates practices that elsewhere belong to haṭha treatises."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B1.4","title":"The Fifteen-Limb Yoga concluded (limbs 11–15)","sa":["प्राणसंयमः प्रत्याहारो धारणा आत्मध्यानं समाधिश्चेति तृतीयाङ्गानि । एतैः पञ्चदशाङ्गैस्तारकं साधयेत् ॥"],"iast":["prāṇa-saṃyamaḥ pratyāhāro dhāraṇā ātma-dhyānaṃ samādhiś ceti tṛtīya-aṅgāni / etaiḥ pañcadaśa-aṅgais tārakaṃ sādhayet //"],"words":[["prāṇa-saṃyamaḥ","","restraint of prāṇa"],["pratyāhāraḥ","","withdrawal of senses"],["dhāraṇā","","concentration"],["ātma-dhyānam","","contemplation of the Self"],["samādhiḥ ca iti","","and absorption — (these are) the third (set of limbs)"],["etaiḥ pañcadaśa-aṅgaiḥ","","by these fifteen limbs"],["tārakaṃ sādhayet","","one should accomplish the tāraka"]],"lit":"(Limbs 11 through 15:) prāṇa-saṃyama (restraint of prāṇa), pratyāhāra (withdrawal of the senses), dhāraṇā (concentration), ātma-dhyāna (Self-contemplation), and samādhi. By these fifteen limbs, the tāraka should be accomplished.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The final five correspond to Patañjali’s prāṇāyāma, pratyāhāra, dhāraṇā, dhyāna, samādhi — with the crucial substitution: dhyāna is specifically ātma-dhyāna, contemplation of the Self, not contemplation of any object. This anchors the practice in advaita rather than yogācāra."],["Ayyangar","Notes that ātma-dhyāna replaces what in Patañjali is generic dhyāna; the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa thereby forecloses object-meditation as the path’s climax."],["Sivananda","Practical sequencing: the first five build moral ground; the next five build physical ground; the last five build inner ground."],["Krishnananda","The fifteen are not parallel options but a sequence; samādhi is the natural outcome of ātma-dhyāna, which itself is the outcome of dhāraṇā established on the foundation of pratyāhāra and prāṇa-saṃyama."],["Deussen","Recognises the formal parallel to Patañjali but emphasises the philosophical difference: this is jñāna-yoga embedded in haṭha and rāja techniques, not a separate path."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B1.5","title":"The Three Lakṣyas Introduced","sa":["अन्तर्लक्ष्यं बहिर्लक्ष्यं मध्यलक्ष्यं चेति त्रिविधं तारकलक्ष्यम् । अन्तर्लक्ष्यं तु अन्तर्ज्योतिः सुषुम्नागतम् । बहिर्लक्ष्यं तु बहिराकाशे नीलादिप्रकाशम् । मध्यलक्ष्यं तु चिदाकाशसाक्षाद्भूतम् ॥"],"iast":["antar-lakṣyaṃ bahir-lakṣyaṃ madhya-lakṣyaṃ ceti tri-vidhaṃ tāraka-lakṣyam / antar-lakṣyaṃ tu antar-jyotiḥ suṣumnā-gatam / bahir-lakṣyaṃ tu bahir-ākāśe nīla-ādi-prakāśam / madhya-lakṣyaṃ tu cid-ākāśa-sākṣād-bhūtam //"],"words":[["antar-lakṣyaṃ","","the internal target"],["bahir-lakṣyaṃ","","the external target"],["madhya-lakṣyaṃ ca iti","","and the middle target"],["tri-vidhaṃ tāraka-lakṣyam","","— the threefold target of the tāraka"],["antar-lakṣyaṃ tu","","the internal target, indeed"],["antar-jyotiḥ","","the inner light"],["suṣumnā-gatam","","placed in the suṣumnā"],["bahir-lakṣyaṃ tu","","the external target, indeed"],["bahir-ākāśe","","in the external space"],["nīla-ādi-prakāśam","","the radiance beginning with blue (the colour-spaces)"],["madhya-lakṣyaṃ tu","","the middle target, indeed"],["cid-ākāśa-sākṣād-bhūtam","","— the direct presence of the cid-ākāśa (conscious space)"]],"lit":"The threefold target of the tāraka comprises the internal, external, and middle targets. The internal is the inner light placed in the suṣumnā. The external is the radiance beginning with blue in the external space. The middle is the direct presence of the cid-ākāśa.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The three-lakṣya scheme of Advaya-tāraka here receives its first elaboration. Brahma-Yogin emphasises “direct presence” (sākṣād-bhūtam) for the middle target: this is not contemplation OF cid-ākāśa but recognition IN it."],["Ayyangar","Translates cid-ākāśa-sākṣād-bhūtam as “experienced as the conscious ether” — the central practice of madhya-lakṣya."],["Sivananda","Locates the three lakṣyas in three practical phases: closed-eye antar, open-eye bahir, eyes-half-closed madhya — all to be practiced in one sitting."],["Krishnananda","The three are not three objects but three modes of the antaḥkaraṇa, as taught in the Advaya-tāraka. The Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa here recapitulates that doctrine and prepares its expansion in Brāhmaṇa II."],["Deussen","Recognises this as the locus where the three-lakṣya doctrine becomes a complete practice manual; Advaya-tāraka stated the doctrine, Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa instructs in it."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Brāhmaṇa II  ·  Tāraka-Yoga & the Three Lakṣyas","u":[{"ref":"B2.1","title":"Tāraka Twofold — Pūrva (with form) and Amanaska (without form)","sa":["अथ हैनं याज्ञवल्क्यो भगवन्तमादित्यं पप्रच्छ — तारकयोगस्य स्वरूपं ब्रूहीति । स होवाच — तारकं द्विविधं पूर्वमुत्तरं चेति । मूर्तितारकं पूर्वम् अमूर्तितारकमुत्तरं तदेवामनस्कमित्युच्यते ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ yājñavalkyo bhagavantam ādityaṃ papraccha — tāraka-yogasya svarūpaṃ brūhīti / sa hovāca — tārakaṃ dvividhaṃ pūrvam uttaraṃ ceti / mūrti-tārakaṃ pūrvam a-mūrti-tārakam uttaraṃ tad eva amanaskam ity ucyate //"],"words":[["atha hainaṃ yājñavalkyaḥ","","now then this Yājñavalkya"],["bhagavantam ādityaṃ papraccha","","asked the Lord Āditya"],["tāraka-yogasya svarūpaṃ brūhi iti","","“declare the nature of tāraka-yoga”"],["saḥ ha uvāca","","he (Āditya) said"],["tārakaṃ dvividhaṃ","","tāraka is twofold"],["pūrvam uttaraṃ ca iti","","— the earlier and the later"],["mūrti-tārakam pūrvam","","tāraka-with-form is the earlier"],["a-mūrti-tārakam uttaram","","tāraka-without-form is the later"],["tat eva amanaskam iti ucyate","","that very one is called the “no-mind”"]],"lit":"Then Yājñavalkya asked Lord Āditya: “Declare the nature of tāraka-yoga.” He replied: “Tāraka is twofold — the earlier and the later. Tāraka-with-form is the earlier; tāraka-without-form is the later, and that itself is called amanaska (no-mind).”","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The same twofold scheme as Advaya-tāraka mantra 3 — but here developed at length. Brahma-Yogin notes: the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa is the operating manual for what Advaya-tāraka states aphoristically. The uttara-tāraka / amanaska identity is the key: the second stage has no separate technique."],["Ayyangar","Translates mūrti-tāraka as “tāraka having form” and a-mūrti-tāraka as “tāraka without form,” preserving the asymmetry: one is a contemplation, the other is the cessation of contemplation."],["Sivananda","The practical reading: pūrva-tāraka is the active phase — gaze, breath, posture, inner image; uttara-tāraka is what arrives when the practitioner releases the active phase. Effort cannot reach it; effort can only prepare for it."],["Krishnananda","Reads the two together as one continuous process: mūrti-tāraka is upāya (means); a-mūrti-tāraka is upeya (end). The relation is not sequential in time but ontological — the means is already the end provisionally."],["Deussen","The amanaska doctrine here in nuce; it will be fully elaborated in the later Amanaska Yoga and the Yoga-vāsiṣṭha. The Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa marks an important stage in this doctrine’s history."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B2.2","title":"Antar-Lakṣya Elaborated","sa":["अन्तर्लक्ष्यं तु अष्टदलकमलमध्ये कोटिसूर्यप्रकाशं हृदयाकाशं ज्योतिर्मयं ब्रह्मस्वरूपम् । तस्मिन् दृष्ट्या मनो लीयते सा अन्तर्लक्ष्यलक्षणावस्था ॥"],"iast":["antar-lakṣyaṃ tu aṣṭa-dala-kamala-madhye koṭi-sūrya-prakāśaṃ hṛdaya-ākāśaṃ jyotir-mayaṃ brahma-svarūpam / tasmin dṛṣṭyā mano līyate sā antar-lakṣya-lakṣaṇa-avasthā //"],"words":[["antar-lakṣyaṃ tu","","the internal target, indeed"],["aṣṭa-dala-kamala-madhye","","in the middle of the eight-petalled lotus (the heart)"],["koṭi-sūrya-prakāśaṃ","","shining as ten million suns"],["hṛdaya-ākāśaṃ","","— the heart-space"],["jyotir-mayaṃ","","made of light"],["brahma-svarūpam","","of the very form of brahman"],["tasmin","","on that"],["dṛṣṭyā mano līyate","","by (such) seeing the mind dissolves"],["sā","","that is"],["antar-lakṣya-lakṣaṇa-avasthā","","the state defined as the internal target"]],"lit":"The internal target is the heart-space within the eight-petalled lotus, shining as ten million suns, made of light, of the very form of brahman. By such seeing the mind dissolves — that is the state defined as the internal target.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","Locates antar-lakṣya precisely at the eight-petalled lotus of the heart (anāhata cakra). The image of koṭi-sūrya-prakāśa is not metaphor — it is the actual appearance to the mature practitioner."],["Ayyangar","Notes the eight petals: the directions of the heart-lotus correspond to the eight bhāvas of the buddhi (Sāṃkhya’s dharma-jñāna-vairāgya-aiśvarya and their opposites)."],["Sivananda","Practical: visualise the eight-petalled lotus first; within it, the light appears spontaneously. Forced visualisation of the light without the lotus rarely yields stable seeing."],["Krishnananda","The criterion mano līyate (the mind dissolves) is critical — not “the mind sees the light,” but “the mind dissolves into the light.” The first is still duality; the second is laya."],["Deussen","Compares the heart-lotus image to Chāndogya 8.1 (the dahara-vidyā); the same cosmology, made operational in yogic terms here."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B2.3","title":"Bahir-Lakṣya Elaborated","sa":["बहिर्लक्ष्यं तु नासाग्रे चतुर्भिः षड्भिर्अष्टभिर्दशभिः द्वादशभिश्च क्रमशो अङ्गुलैः नीलश्यामरक्तपीतशुक्ल-आकाशवर्णैः बहिराकाशे प्रकाशदर्शनम् । तदेव वैष्णवं परं पदम् ॥"],"iast":["bahir-lakṣyaṃ tu nāsāgre caturbhiḥ ṣaḍbhiḥ aṣṭabhiḥ daśabhiḥ dvādaśabhiḥ ca kramaśo aṅgulaiḥ nīla-śyāma-rakta-pīta-śukla-ākāśa-varṇaiḥ bahir-ākāśe prakāśa-darśanam / tad eva vaiṣṇavaṃ paraṃ padam //"],"words":[["bahir-lakṣyaṃ tu","","the external target, indeed"],["nāsāgre","","in front of the nose"],["caturbhiḥ ṣaḍbhiḥ aṣṭabhiḥ daśabhiḥ dvādaśabhiḥ ca","","at 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12"],["kramaśaḥ aṅgulaiḥ","","progressive finger-widths"],["nīla-śyāma-rakta-pīta-śukla-ākāśa-varṇaiḥ","","in the colours blue, dark, red, yellow, white, and sky-coloured"],["bahir-ākāśe prakāśa-darśanam","","— the seeing of light in the external space"],["tat eva vaiṣṇavaṃ paraṃ padam","","— that very (light) is the supreme abode of Viṣṇu"]],"lit":"The external target is the seeing of light in the external space at four, six, eight, ten, and twelve finger-widths in front of the nose, in the colours blue, dark, red, yellow, white, and sky-coloured. That very (light) is the supreme abode of Viṣṇu (Vaiṣṇavaṃ paraṃ padam).","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","Exact parallel with Advaya-tāraka mantra 7. Brahma-Yogin notes: the colours arise of themselves to the steady gaze; they are not visualised. The identification with the Vaiṣṇavaṃ paraṃ padam (Ṛg-veda 1.22.20) bridges Vedic sūkta and yoga technique."],["Ayyangar","Notes the finger-width measure (aṅgula) is a personal measure: the practitioner’s own finger-width is the unit. Hence the spaces are body-relative."],["Sivananda","In open-eye practice, the practitioner gazes softly into space about a metre ahead; the colour-spaces will appear after some sittings. Do not strain."],["Krishnananda","The external target is the same light as the internal — the difference is only in the stance of perception, not in the object."],["Deussen","The Ṛg-vedic citation (tad viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam, RV 1.22.20) shows the Upaniṣad’s self-conscious integration of Vedic mantra into yogic practice — a pattern of late-Vedic synthesis."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B2.4","title":"Madhya-Lakṣya — Five Forms of Light in the Cid-Ākāśa","sa":["मध्यलक्ष्यं तु प्रातःकालादिमारब्ध्य सहस्रसूर्यसन्निभं प्रथमं चित्राकाशम् । तत्र चिदाकाशं तत्र परमाकाशं तत्र महाकाशं तत्र सूर्याकाशं चेति पञ्चाकाशदर्शनम् । तत्र चिदाकाशसाक्षाद्भूतावस्था मध्यलक्ष्यलक्षणम् ॥"],"iast":["madhya-lakṣyaṃ tu prātaḥ-kāla-ādim ārabhya sahasra-sūrya-sannibhaṃ prathamaṃ citra-ākāśam / tatra cid-ākāśaṃ tatra parama-ākāśaṃ tatra mahā-ākāśaṃ tatra sūrya-ākāśaṃ ca iti pañca-ākāśa-darśanam / tatra cid-ākāśa-sākṣād-bhūta-avasthā madhya-lakṣya-lakṣaṇam //"],"words":[["madhya-lakṣyaṃ tu","","the middle target, indeed"],["prātaḥ-kāla-ādim ārabhya","","beginning from dawn-time onward"],["sahasra-sūrya-sannibhaṃ","","like a thousand suns"],["prathamaṃ citra-ākāśam","","— first, the variegated space"],["tatra cid-ākāśaṃ","","therein the conscious space"],["tatra parama-ākāśaṃ","","therein the supreme space"],["tatra mahā-ākāśaṃ","","therein the great space"],["tatra sūrya-ākāśaṃ ca iti","","and therein the sun-space, thus"],["pañca-ākāśa-darśanam","","— the five-space seeing"],["tatra","","therein"],["cid-ākāśa-sākṣād-bhūta-avasthā","","the state in which the cid-ākāśa is directly present"],["madhya-lakṣya-lakṣaṇam","","— is the mark of the middle target"]],"lit":"The middle target, beginning from dawn-time, is first the variegated space (citra-ākāśa) shining like a thousand suns; within it the conscious space (cid-ākāśa), within that the supreme space (parama-ākāśa), within that the great space (mahā-ākāśa), and within that the sun-space (sūrya-ākāśa) — these are the five spaces. The state in which the cid-ākāśa is directly present is the mark of the middle target.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The five ākāśas are introduced here, to be elaborated in Brāhmaṇa III. Note the nesting: each space is within the previous. They are not five separate ākāśas but five depths of one ākāśa."],["Ayyangar","Translates the nesting carefully: “in that, in that, in that…” The English preserves the contemplative move from surface to depth."],["Sivananda","Best practiced at prātaḥ-kāla (the brāhma-muhūrta, 4:30–6:00 am); the discriminations are difficult to make at other times."],["Krishnananda","The five-fold ākāśa is the practical correlate of the doctrinal panca-koṣa (five-sheath) analysis — but here read inward through luminosity rather than outward through embodiment."],["Deussen","The classification is unique to this Upaniṣad and to its near-cognate Advaya-tāraka. Within the Western reception, this is the locus that has most engaged students of comparative mysticism."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Brāhmaṇa III  ·  The Five Ākāśas","u":[{"ref":"B3.1","title":"The Five Ākāśas Enumerated","sa":["अथ हैनं याज्ञवल्क्यो भगवन्तमादित्यं पञ्चाकाशस्वरूपं ब्रूहीति । स होवाच — आकाशः परमाकाशो महाकाशः सूर्याकाशः परमाकाशश्चेति । अथवा आकाशः परः सूर्यश्च परम इति पञ्चाकाशा आत्मरूपाः ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ yājñavalkyo bhagavantam ādityaṃ pañca-ākāśa-svarūpaṃ brūhīti / sa hovāca — ākāśaḥ parama-ākāśaḥ mahā-ākāśaḥ sūrya-ākāśaḥ parama-ākāśaś ceti / athavā ākāśaḥ paraḥ sūryaś ca parama iti pañca-ākāśā ātma-rūpāḥ //"],"words":[["atha hainaṃ yājñavalkyaḥ","","now then this Yājñavalkya"],["bhagavantam ādityaṃ","","asked Lord Āditya"],["pañca-ākāśa-svarūpaṃ brūhi iti","","“declare the nature of the five spaces”"],["saḥ ha uvāca","","he said"],["ākāśaḥ","","space"],["parama-ākāśaḥ","","supreme space"],["mahā-ākāśaḥ","","great space"],["sūrya-ākāśaḥ","","sun-space"],["parama-ākāśaḥ ca iti","","and the highest space"],["athavā","","or"],["ākāśaḥ paraḥ sūryaḥ ca paramaḥ iti","","— (named) space, supreme, great, sun, highest"],["pañca-ākāśāḥ ātma-rūpāḥ","","— the five spaces, all forms of the Self"]],"lit":"Then Yājñavalkya asked Lord Āditya: “Declare the nature of the five ākāśas.” He replied: “Ākāśa, parama-ākāśa, mahā-ākāśa, sūrya-ākāśa, and parama-ākāśa (the highest). These five spaces are all forms of the Self.”","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The standard enumeration. Some manuscripts read tattva-ākāśa for one of the five; the doctrine is unaffected. Brahma-Yogin emphasises ātma-rūpāḥ — all five are forms of the Self, not five different objects."],["Ayyangar","Notes the manuscript variant: some editions list as ākāśa, parākāśa, mahā-ākāśa, tattva-ākāśa, sūrya-ākāśa. The five-fold count is invariant; the names slightly vary. Adyar follows the reading given here."],["Sivananda","The five ākāśas are five levels of inner space — each subtler than the last, each containing the previous as its substrate."],["Krishnananda","These are five depths of contemplation, not five spatial regions. The yogi penetrates from the outermost (ākāśa proper) to the innermost (parama-ākāśa) through successive deepening."],["Deussen","A unique classification, found in fully developed form only here and in the cognate Yoga Upaniṣads. Compares to Plotinus’ five hypostases as a contemplative cosmography."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B3.2","title":"Ākāśa — The First (Outermost) Space","sa":["अन्धकारावृत आकाशो भवति । तत्र चक्षुर्मीलयित्वा यः कश्चिद्दृश्यते स आकाशः ॥"],"iast":["andha-kāra-āvṛta ākāśo bhavati / tatra cakṣur mīlayitvā yaḥ kaścid dṛśyate sa ākāśaḥ //"],"words":[["andha-kāra-āvṛtaḥ","","covered with darkness"],["ākāśaḥ bhavati","","is (this first) ākāśa"],["tatra","","therein"],["cakṣuḥ mīlayitvā","","having closed the eyes"],["yaḥ kaścid dṛśyate","","whatever is seen"],["saḥ ākāśaḥ","","is (this) ākāśa"]],"lit":"The first ākāśa is covered with darkness. Closing the eyes, whatever is seen — that is this ākāśa.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The starting condition: eyes closed, awareness aware of inner darkness. This is the entry into contemplation; the darkness itself is the first ākāśa."],["Ayyangar","Renders “whatever is seen” deliberately — the practitioner is not yet discriminating; bare awareness of dark inner space is sufficient."],["Sivananda","Begin every sitting here. Without first stabilising in this first ākāśa, the subtler ones cannot be discriminated."],["Krishnananda","The very ordinariness of this first ākāśa is the point — yoga begins not with extraordinary states but with disciplined attention to the most ordinary one."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B3.3","title":"Parākāśa and Mahā-Ākāśa","sa":["पराकाशस्तु शान्तज्योतिःस्वरूपः सर्वाधारः सर्ववस्तुप्रकाशकः । महाकाशस्तु कालाग्निसमप्रभः अकालः सर्वलक्ष्यवर्जितः ॥"],"iast":["para-ākāśas tu śānta-jyotiḥ-svarūpaḥ sarva-ādhāraḥ sarva-vastu-prakāśakaḥ / mahā-ākāśas tu kāla-agni-sama-prabhaḥ a-kālaḥ sarva-lakṣya-varjitaḥ //"],"words":[["para-ākāśaḥ tu","","the supreme space, indeed"],["śānta-jyotiḥ-svarūpaḥ","","of the nature of peaceful light"],["sarva-ādhāraḥ","","the substrate of all"],["sarva-vastu-prakāśakaḥ","","the illuminator of all things"],["mahā-ākāśaḥ tu","","the great space, indeed"],["kāla-agni-sama-prabhaḥ","","of brightness like the cosmic fire of dissolution"],["a-kālaḥ","","time-less"],["sarva-lakṣya-varjitaḥ","","free of any (definite) target"]],"lit":"Parākāśa is of the nature of peaceful light — the substrate of all, the illuminator of all things. Mahākāśa is bright as the fire of cosmic dissolution, timeless, free of any (definite) target.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The two together: parākāśa is the calm substrate (śānta-jyotis); mahākāśa is its dynamic aspect (kāla-agni-prabha). The first is recognised in stillness; the second appears in the dissolving of all images."],["Ayyangar","Kāla-agni is the fire at the time of pralaya; the contemplation suggests an overwhelming radiance in which all forms are consumed."],["Sivananda","In practice, parākāśa is the experience of a steady inner light; mahākāśa arises when even that steady light expands into an unbounded brilliance."],["Krishnananda","Sarva-lakṣya-varjita is the crucial marker: at this depth, there is no longer any “target” of contemplation — the contemplator has been absorbed into what was contemplated."],["Deussen","Notes the philosophical move: from objects (in citra-ākāśa) to substrate-light (parākāśa) to lakṣya-less radiance (mahākāśa) is the move from duality to pre-dual awareness."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B3.4","title":"Sūryākāśa — The Sun-Space","sa":["सूर्याकाशस्तु कोटिसूर्यप्रतिमप्रभः । स अहोरात्रवर्जितः । तत्र दृष्ट्या एकीभूतस्य योगिनो विदेहमुक्तिर्भवति ॥"],"iast":["sūrya-ākāśas tu koṭi-sūrya-pratima-prabhaḥ / sa aho-rātra-varjitaḥ / tatra dṛṣṭyā ekī-bhūtasya yogino videha-muktir bhavati //"],"words":[["sūrya-ākāśaḥ tu","","the sun-space, indeed"],["koṭi-sūrya-pratima-prabhaḥ","","of brightness comparable to ten million suns"],["saḥ aho-rātra-varjitaḥ","","free of day-and-night (i.e., timeless)"],["tatra","","therein"],["dṛṣṭyā ekī-bhūtasya","","of (the yogi) who has become one through (such) seeing"],["yoginaḥ","","of the yogi"],["videha-muktiḥ bhavati","","liberation-without-body arises"]],"lit":"Sūryākāśa is of brightness comparable to ten million suns. It is beyond day and night. For the yogi who has become one through seeing therein, liberation-without-body (videha-mukti) arises.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The decisive shift: at sūryākāśa, the seeing itself becomes identity (ekī-bhūta). The result is videha-mukti — release that does not require death but is not bound to the body either."],["Ayyangar","Renders ekī-bhūta as “become one” — the subject-object distinction is dissolved in the act of seeing."],["Sivananda","The most advanced stage in this sequence; few reach it. Most practitioners oscillate among the first three ākāśas; sūryākāśa marks the threshold of mukti."],["Krishnananda","The phrase aho-rātra-varjita is theologically loaded: this is the time-less reality of Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4 (yatra naktaṃ na divā na sadāsac cobhayam …), expressed here as a contemplative state."],["Deussen","Notes the precise locution “videha-muktiḥ bhavati” — this is one of the earliest Upaniṣadic uses of the technical term videha-mukti, which Vedānta later systematises against jīvanmukti."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B3.5","title":"Paramākāśa — The Highest Space","sa":["परमाकाशस्तु अनिर्वाच्यनिजानन्दप्रकाशो ब्रह्मस्वरूपः परिपूर्णोऽद्वितीयः । तस्मिन् साक्षात्कारेण योगी कैवल्यपदं प्राप्नोति ॥"],"iast":["parama-ākāśas tu a-nirvācya-nija-ānanda-prakāśo brahma-svarūpaḥ paripūrṇo’dvitīyaḥ / tasmin sākṣātkāreṇa yogī kaivalya-padaṃ prāpnoti //"],"words":[["parama-ākāśaḥ tu","","the highest space, indeed"],["a-nirvācya","","inexpressible"],["nija-ānanda-prakāśaḥ","","of intrinsic bliss-radiance"],["brahma-svarūpaḥ","","of the very form of brahman"],["paripūrṇaḥ","","wholly full"],["a-dvitīyaḥ","","non-dual"],["tasmin sākṣātkāreṇa","","by direct realisation in that"],["yogī","","the yogi"],["kaivalya-padaṃ prāpnoti","","attains the abode of isolation/aloneness (kaivalya)"]],"lit":"Paramākāśa is the inexpressible radiance of intrinsic bliss — of the very form of brahman, wholly full, non-dual. By direct realisation in that, the yogi attains kaivalya.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The pinnacle. Note the term kaivalya — borrowed from Sāṃkhya/Yoga but glossed advaitically: not the isolation of puruṣa from prakṛti, but the recognition that brahman is alone."],["Ayyangar","Translates a-nirvācya carefully as “inexpressible” — not merely undefined but beyond the reach of speech (yato vāco nivartante, Taittirīya 2.4)."],["Sivananda","The crown of the five-ākāśa practice. Here words stop; the practitioner only rests in what has been recognised."],["Krishnananda","Paripūrṇaḥ advitīyaḥ are the standard advaitic predicates of brahman — this Upaniṣad here aligns its yoga unmistakably with Vedānta."],["Deussen","The final ākāśa is identical with the brahman of the principal Upaniṣads; the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa’s synthesis is complete here."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Brāhmaṇa IV  ·  Doṣas, Lakṣaṇas, the Nine Cakras, and the Jīvanmukta","u":[{"ref":"B4.1","title":"The Five Doṣas (Obstacles to be Overcome)","sa":["अथ हैनं याज्ञवल्क्यः पप्रच्छ — दोषाणां स्वरूपं ब्रूहीति । स होवाच — कामक्रोधनिःश्वासभयनिद्रालस्या इति पञ्च योगिनो दोषाः । एते योगाभ्यासेन निवर्तन्ते ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ yājñavalkyaḥ papraccha — doṣāṇāṃ svarūpaṃ brūhīti / sa hovāca — kāma-krodha-niḥśvāsa-bhaya-nidrā-ālasyā iti pañca yogino doṣāḥ / ete yoga-abhyāsena nivartante //"],"words":[["atha hainaṃ yājñavalkyaḥ papraccha","","now then Yājñavalkya asked"],["doṣāṇāṃ svarūpaṃ brūhi iti","","“declare the nature of the (yogi’s) defects”"],["saḥ ha uvāca","","he said"],["kāma","","desire"],["krodha","","anger"],["niḥśvāsa","","(heavy) breathing / sighing"],["bhaya","","fear"],["nidrā-ālasyāḥ","","sleep and lethargy"],["iti pañca yoginaḥ doṣāḥ","","these are the five defects of the yogi"],["ete yoga-abhyāsena nivartante","","— they are removed by yoga-practice"]],"lit":"Then Yājñavalkya asked: “Declare the nature of the (yogi’s) defects.” He replied: “Desire, anger, irregular breathing, fear, and sleep-with-lethargy — these are the five defects of the yogi. They are removed by yoga-practice.”","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","A diagnostic list. Brahma-Yogin notes that the five are progressively subtler: kāma is the most overt; ālasya the most disguised. The phrase yoga-abhyāsena nivartante is operationally precise: not philosophical inquiry, not ritual, but sustained yogic practice removes them."],["Ayyangar","Pairs nidrā-ālasya as a compound; the two are mutually reinforcing and have to be treated together."],["Sivananda","Each defect has its specific antidote: kāma → vairāgya; krodha → kṣamā; niḥśvāsa → prāṇāyāma; bhaya → ātma-jñāna; nidrā-ālasya → sāttvic diet + brāhma-muhūrta rising."],["Krishnananda","These five are obstacles to dhāraṇā, not to āsana or prāṇāyāma alone. They impede the deeper limbs; the earlier limbs can proceed alongside them, which is why beginners do not notice them."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B4.2","title":"The Lakṣaṇas (Signs of Progress)","sa":["योगसिद्धेर्लक्षणानि — अनामयत्वम् अलोलुपत्वम् इन्द्रियजयो दृढासनसिद्धिः ध्यानस्थैर्यं चेति । एते सिद्धस्य योगिनो ज्ञेयाः ॥"],"iast":["yoga-siddher lakṣaṇāni — anāmayatvam alolupatvam indriya-jayo dṛḍha-āsana-siddhiḥ dhyāna-sthairyaṃ ca iti / ete siddhasya yogino jñeyāḥ //"],"words":[["yoga-siddheḥ","","of the perfection of yoga"],["lakṣaṇāni","","are the signs"],["anāmayatvam","","freedom from illness"],["alolupatvam","","absence of craving"],["indriya-jayaḥ","","mastery of the senses"],["dṛḍha-āsana-siddhiḥ","","accomplishment of firm posture"],["dhyāna-sthairyam ca iti","","and steadiness in contemplation"],["ete","","these are"],["siddhasya yoginaḥ jñeyāḥ","","(the signs) to be known of the accomplished yogi"]],"lit":"The signs of yoga-accomplishment are: freedom from illness, absence of craving, mastery of the senses, accomplishment of firm posture, and steadiness in contemplation. These are to be known of the accomplished yogi.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","A diagnostic counter-list to the doṣas. Where the five doṣas obstruct, these five mark their removal. Anāmayatvam is not absolute freedom from disease but freedom from the impingement of disease on practice."],["Ayyangar","Renders alolupatvam as “absence of greed”; the broader sense is the cessation of grasping toward objects."],["Sivananda","Practical: check yourself against these five periodically. If illness frequently interrupts sitting, or if craving is undiminished, the earlier limbs need more work; do not advance."],["Krishnananda","These are not yogic supernormal powers (siddhis in the technical sense) but the natural functional signs of a settled inner life — the prerequisites for the higher contemplations."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B4.3","title":"The Nine Cakras","sa":["मूलाधारः स्वाधिष्ठानं मणिपूरमनाहतं विशुद्धं ललना आज्ञा मनश्चक्रं सोमचक्रं चेति नव चक्राणि । तेषु धारणयोपासनेन योगी सिद्ध्यति ॥"],"iast":["mūlādhāraḥ svādhiṣṭhānaṃ maṇi-pūram anāhataṃ viśuddhaṃ lalanā ājñā manaś-cakraṃ soma-cakraṃ ca iti nava cakrāṇi / teṣu dhāraṇayā upāsanena yogī sidhyati //"],"words":[["mūlādhāraḥ","","root-support (perineum)"],["svādhiṣṭhānam","","own-base (genital region)"],["maṇi-pūram","","jewel-city (navel)"],["anāhatam","","unstruck (heart)"],["viśuddham","","pure (throat)"],["lalanā","","lalanā (root of soft palate / uvular region)"],["ājñā","","command (between brows)"],["manaḥ-cakram","","mind-cakra (above ājñā)"],["soma-cakraṃ ca iti","","and moon-cakra"],["nava cakrāṇi","","— the nine cakras"],["teṣu","","in these"],["dhāraṇayā upāsanena","","by concentration and contemplation"],["yogī sidhyati","","the yogi is perfected"]],"lit":"Mūlādhāra, svādhiṣṭhāna, maṇipūra, anāhata, viśuddha, lalanā, ājñā, manas-cakra, and soma-cakra — these are the nine cakras. By concentration and contemplation in these, the yogi is perfected.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","A nine-fold cakra scheme rather than the standard six. The three additional are lalanā (at the root of the soft palate, between viśuddha and ājñā), manas-cakra (above ājñā), and soma-cakra (in the region of the sahasrāra). These three are subtle stations along the same suṣumnā."],["Ayyangar","Notes that this nine-fold scheme is rare in the Upaniṣad corpus; closely paralleled in the Yoga-cūḍāmaṇi and the later tantric handbooks."],["Sivananda","The three additional cakras (lalanā, manas, soma) are stations of subtle practice once the first six are stabilised; do not approach them without the foundation."],["Krishnananda","The nine-fold scheme indicates that the standard six-cakra map is an abbreviation; finer practice distinguishes more stations. The principle is always the same: dhāraṇā at each station yields a specific result."],["Deussen","Compares with the Tantric nine-cakra schemes (also found in the Śrīcakra tradition); the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa is an early Upaniṣadic witness to this finer taxonomy."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B4.4","title":"The State of the Jīvanmukta","sa":["य एवं वेद स जीवन्मुक्तो भवति । अचलः स्थिरमतिर्निःस्पृहो निर्ममो निरहंकारो द्वन्द्वातीतो ब्रह्मस्वरूपः । देहयात्रामात्रवशिष्टः ॥"],"iast":["ya evaṃ veda sa jīvan-mukto bhavati / acalaḥ sthira-matir niḥspṛho nirmamo nir-ahaṃkāro dvandva-atīto brahma-svarūpaḥ / deha-yātrā-mātra-vaśiṣṭaḥ //"],"words":[["yaḥ evaṃ veda","","whoever knows thus"],["saḥ jīvan-muktaḥ bhavati","","becomes liberated-while-living"],["acalaḥ","","unmoved"],["sthira-matiḥ","","of steady mind"],["niḥspṛhaḥ","","without longing"],["nir-mamaḥ","","without \"mine\""],["nir-ahaṃkāraḥ","","without \"I\""],["dvandva-atītaḥ","","transcending the opposites"],["brahma-svarūpaḥ","","of the very form of brahman"],["deha-yātrā-mātra-vaśiṣṭaḥ","","— only the bare maintenance of the body remains"]],"lit":"Whoever knows thus becomes liberated-while-living (jīvanmukta) — unmoved, of steady mind, without longing, without \"mine\", without \"I\", transcending the opposites, of the very form of brahman; only the bare maintenance of the body remains.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","A precise definition. Note the seven attributes: acala, sthira-mati, niḥspṛha, nirmama, nirahaṃkāra, dvandva-atīta, brahma-svarūpa. The eighth phrase deha-yātrā-mātra-vaśiṣṭa names the residue: the body continues by inertia, but no new identification arises."],["Ayyangar","Renders deha-yātrā-mātra-vaśiṣṭa as “only the journey of the body remains” — the metaphor is yātrā (journey), with the implication that the journey will complete itself naturally."],["Sivananda","These seven are the diagnostic of jīvanmukti. They are not aspirations but facts about the liberated one; anyone who has to strive after them is not yet there."],["Krishnananda","The phrase nirmamo nirahaṃkāra is the precise echo of Bhagavad-gītā 2.71 (nirmamo nirahaṃkāraḥ sa śāntim adhigacchati). The Upaniṣad here is making the same identification."],["Deussen","One of the clearest Upaniṣadic articulations of jīvanmukti; the doctrine here is fully formed, anticipating Vidyāraṇya’s Jīvanmukti-viveka by some centuries."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Brāhmaṇa V  ·  Videhamukti and the Supreme State","u":[{"ref":"B5.1","title":"Videhamukti — Liberation Without Body","sa":["देहयात्रासमाप्तौ प्रारब्धक्षये यस्य ब्रह्मसाक्षात्कारेण मुक्तस्य देहत्यागे न पुनरावृत्तिः सा विदेहमुक्तिः । तत्र न कश्चिल्लक्ष्यो न दृष्टा न दर्शनम् । केवलं ब्रह्मैव शिष्यते ॥"],"iast":["deha-yātrā-samāptau prārabdha-kṣaye yasya brahma-sākṣātkāreṇa muktasya deha-tyāge na punar-āvṛttiḥ sā videha-muktiḥ / tatra na kaścil lakṣyo na draṣṭā na darśanam / kevalaṃ brahma eva śiṣyate //"],"words":[["deha-yātrā-samāptau","","at the completion of the body’s journey"],["prārabdha-kṣaye","","at the exhaustion of (the karma) already begun"],["yasya","","whose (i.e., for whom)"],["brahma-sākṣātkāreṇa muktasya","","liberated by direct realisation of brahman"],["deha-tyāge","","at the casting off of the body"],["na punar-āvṛttiḥ","","(there is) no return"],["sā videha-muktiḥ","","— that is videha-mukti"],["tatra","","therein"],["na kaścit lakṣyaḥ","","no target"],["na draṣṭā","","no seer"],["na darśanam","","no seeing"],["kevalaṃ brahma eva śiṣyate","","brahman alone remains"]],"lit":"At the completion of the body’s journey, at the exhaustion of prārabdha karma, when the one liberated by direct realisation of brahman casts off the body, there is no return — that is videha-mukti. Therein there is no target, no seer, no seeing. Brahman alone remains.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The classical distinction between jīvanmukti and videhamukti. The Upaniṣad is precise: jīvanmukti is described in Brāhmaṇa IV (the body remains); videhamukti is described here (the body has gone). The same realisation underlies both; only the bodily condition differs."],["Ayyangar","Renders na lakṣyo na draṣṭā na darśanam in the threefold negation — the triad of seer-seeing-seen that defines all duality is here entirely absent."],["Sivananda","Reads this as instructional: do not expect videhamukti to be a further state arrived at; it is the natural completion of jīvanmukti when the body falls away."],["Krishnananda","The kevalaṃ brahma eva śiṣyate is the absolute statement of the entire Vedānta — the remainder of the whole inquiry, when the temporary structures of identification fall away."],["Deussen","Notes that the prārabdha-kṣaya doctrine here is fully formed; later Vedānta (notably Vidyāraṇya, c. 14th c.) systematises it, but the Upaniṣad already distinguishes prārabdha (acting) from sañcita and āgāmin."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B5.2","title":"The Supreme State — Beyond All Description","sa":["यद्भूतं भव्यं वा सर्वं ब्रह्म । तदद्वैतं तत्परं तत्तारकम् । न तत्र दिनं न रात्रिः न कालः न देशः न लक्ष्यं किञ्चित् । अनुभूतिमात्रसारः ॥"],"iast":["yad bhūtaṃ bhavyaṃ vā sarvaṃ brahma / tad advaitaṃ tat paraṃ tat tārakam / na tatra dinaṃ na rātriḥ na kālaḥ na deśaḥ na lakṣyaṃ kiñcit / anubhūti-mātra-sāraḥ //"],"words":[["yad bhūtaṃ bhavyam vā","","whatever has been or will be"],["sarvaṃ brahma","","— is wholly brahman"],["tat advaitam","","that is non-dual"],["tat param","","that is supreme"],["tat tārakam","","that is the tāraka"],["na tatra dinam","","therein no day"],["na rātriḥ","","no night"],["na kālaḥ","","no time"],["na deśaḥ","","no place"],["na lakṣyaṃ kiñcit","","no target at all"],["anubhūti-mātra-sāraḥ","","— its essence is pure experience alone"]],"lit":"Whatever has been or will be is wholly brahman. That is the non-dual, the supreme, the tāraka. Therein there is no day, no night, no time, no place, no target at all — its essence is pure experience alone.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The summary statement of the entire teaching. Brahma-Yogin notes the compound anubhūti-mātra-sāra: not jñāna (cognition), not vedana (sensing), but anubhūti (direct experience) — the term reserved for the experience that is its own subject."],["Ayyangar","Renders anubhūti-mātra-sāra as “whose essence is pure experience alone” — preserving the technical advaitic sense of anubhūti."],["Sivananda","This verse is for recitation, not analysis. After practice, sit and slowly speak each phrase, allowing it to settle."],["Krishnananda","The triple negation na dinaṃ na rātriḥ na kālaḥ echoes Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4 precisely; the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa is consciously continuing that Upaniṣadic line."],["Deussen","The anubhūti-mātra-sāra is the central term of Indian mysticism in its mature form; the Upaniṣad here gives it operational definition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"B5.3","title":"Phala-śruti — The Declaration of Fruit","sa":["य इमां मण्डलब्राह्मणोपनिषदं नित्यमधीते सोऽग्निपूतो भवति । स वायुपूतो भवति । स आदित्यपूतो भवति । स सर्ववेदपूतो भवति । स सर्वमुनिज्ञानभाग्भवति । स ब्रह्मलोके महीयते । तस्य पुनरावृत्तिर्न विद्यते । इत्युपनिषत् ॥"],"iast":["ya imāṃ maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa-upaniṣadaṃ nityam adhīte so’gni-pūto bhavati / sa vāyu-pūto bhavati / sa āditya-pūto bhavati / sa sarva-veda-pūto bhavati / sa sarva-muni-jñāna-bhāg bhavati / sa brahma-loke mahīyate / tasya punar-āvṛttir na vidyate / ity-upaniṣat //"],"words":[["yaḥ imām maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa-upaniṣadaṃ","","whoever (recites) this Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa Upaniṣad"],["nityam adhīte","","daily studies"],["saḥ agni-pūtaḥ bhavati","","he becomes purified by fire"],["saḥ vāyu-pūtaḥ bhavati","","purified by wind"],["saḥ āditya-pūtaḥ bhavati","","purified by the sun"],["saḥ sarva-veda-pūtaḥ bhavati","","purified by all the Vedas"],["saḥ sarva-muni-jñāna-bhāk bhavati","","shares in the knowledge of all the sages"],["saḥ brahma-loke mahīyate","","is honoured in brahma-loka"],["tasya punar-āvṛttiḥ na vidyate","","for him there is no return"],["iti upaniṣat","","— thus the Upaniṣad"]],"lit":"Whoever daily studies this Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa Upaniṣad becomes purified by fire, wind, and the sun; purified by all the Vedas; shares in the knowledge of all the sages; is honoured in brahma-loka; and for him there is no return. Thus the Upaniṣad.","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","The standard phala-śruti pattern, with the decisive addition: punar-āvṛttiḥ na vidyate — there is no return (to saṃsāra). Brahma-Yogin emphasises that the “no return” clause applies in proportion to the depth of nitya-adhyayana — daily study with comprehension, not mere chanting."],["Ayyangar","Notes the rhythmic structure of the phala-śruti is identical to that in Advaya-tāraka mantra 19; both share the formulaic Yoga-Upaniṣad ending."],["Sivananda","Practical: read or recite Brāhmaṇas I through V every day; the entire text in about 30 minutes. This is the nityam adhīte the phala-śruti speaks of."],["Krishnananda","The closing iti upaniṣat is the formal terminus; it declares the teaching complete in itself. The student is now to take it into life as the daily context of action."],["Deussen","The closing formula is consistent across the Yoga-Upaniṣad corpus; the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa here aligns its conclusion with the genre."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Part IV · Visual Schemata","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Six ASCII diagrams summarising the central architectures of the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa. Monospace-rendered for stability across Word versions."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.1  The Fifteen-Limb Yoga (vs. Patañjali’s Eight)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   │           MAṆḌALA-BRĀHMAṆA  ·  Pañcadaśa-Aṅga-Yoga                  │\n   └─────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                     │\n   ┌─────────────────┴──────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────────────┐\n   │  MORAL/PREPARATORY (1–5)        │    │  PATAÑJALI                   │\n   │   1. yama         restraint     │    │   1. yama                    │\n   │   2. niyama       observance    │    │   2. niyama                  │\n   │   3. tyāga        renunciation  │    │       (implied)              │\n   │   4. mauna        silence       │    │       (implied)              │\n   │   5. deśa         place         │    │       (implied)              │\n   └─────────────────┬──────────────┘    └──────────────────────────────┘\n                     │\n   ┌─────────────────┴──────────────┐\n   │  POSTURAL/PHYSICAL (6–10)       │\n   │   6. kāla         time          │ ◄── implied in Patañjali\n   │   7. āsana        posture       │ ◄── = āsana\n   │   8. mūla-bandha  root-lock     │ ◄── haṭha addition\n   │   9. deha-sāmya   body-evenness │ ◄── haṭha addition\n   │  10. dṛk-sthiti   gaze-fixity   │ ◄── prep. for śāmbhavī\n   └─────────────────┬──────────────┘\n                     │\n   ┌─────────────────┴──────────────┐\n   │  INNER (11–15)                  │\n   │  11. prāṇa-saṃyama   = prāṇāyāma│\n   │  12. pratyāhāra      = pratyāhāra\n   │  13. dhāraṇā         = dhāraṇā  │\n   │  14. ātma-dhyāna     = dhyāna   │ ← restricted to ātman\n   │  15. samādhi         = samādhi  │\n   └────────────────────────────────┘\n\n   Total: 15 limbs (M-B) vs. 8 limbs (Patañjali)\n   Key innovations: tyāga · mauna · deśa · kāla as separate limbs;\n                    mūla-bandha + deha-sāmya + dṛk-sthiti (haṭha);\n                    dhyāna restricted to ātma-dhyāna.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.2  The Three Lakṣyas (Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa expansion)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n                    ┌────────────────────────────┐\n                    │      TĀRAKA-LAKṢYA          │\n                    │       (the three targets)   │\n                    └─────────────┬──────────────┘\n       ┌──────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┐\n       ▼                          ▼                          ▼\n ┌──────────────┐         ┌──────────────┐         ┌──────────────┐\n │  ANTAR       │         │  BAHIR       │         │  MADHYA      │\n │  (inside)    │         │  (in front)  │         │ (chid-ākāśa) │\n ├──────────────┤         ├──────────────┤         ├──────────────┤\n │ aṣṭa-dala-   │         │ at 4 aṅgula: │         │ FIVE ĀKĀŚAS  │\n │  kamala      │         │   nīla       │         │              │\n │ (heart-lotus,│         │ at 6 aṅgula: │         │  1 ākāśa     │\n │  8 petals)   │         │   śyāma      │         │  2 parākāśa  │\n │              │         │ at 8 aṅgula: │         │  3 mahākāśa  │\n │ koṭi-sūrya-  │         │   rakta      │         │  4 sūryākāśa │\n │  prakāśa     │         │ at 10 aṅgula:│         │  5 paramākāśa│\n │              │         │   pīta       │         │              │\n │ &quot;mano līyate&quot;│         │ at 12 aṅgula:│         │ → kaivalya   │\n │  (criterion) │         │   śukla      │         │              │\n │              │         │   + ākāśa-   │         │              │\n │ B2.2         │         │     varṇa    │         │ B2.4 / B3.x  │\n │              │         │ = vaiṣṇavaṃ  │         │              │\n │              │         │   padam      │         │              │\n │              │         │ B2.3         │         │              │\n └──────────────┘         └──────────────┘         └──────────────┘</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.3  The Five Ākāśas (Nested)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   │                                                                │\n   │   ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗      │\n   │   ║  5 · PARAMĀKĀŚA — inexpressible bliss-radiance        ║      │\n   │   ║      brahma-svarūpa · paripūrṇa · advitīya            ║      │\n   │   ║      → KAIVALYA                                      ║      │\n   │   ║                                                       ║      │\n   │   ║   ╔════════════════════════════════════════════════╗ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║  4 · SŪRYĀKĀŚA — koṭi-sūrya-prabhā              ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║      timeless (aho-rātra-varjita)              ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║      → VIDEHA-MUKTI                            ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║                                                ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ╔══════════════════════════════════════════╗ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║  3 · MAHĀKĀŚA — kāla-agni-sama-prabha     ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║      lakṣya-less; dynamic radiance        ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║                                          ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ╔════════════════════════════════════╗ ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║ 2 · PARĀKĀŚA — śānta-jyotis         ║ ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║     substrate, illuminator         ║ ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║                                    ║ ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║  ┌──────────────────────────────┐  ║ ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║  │ 1 · ĀKĀŚA — bare inner dark   │  ║ ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║  │     (eyes closed, whatever    │  ║ ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║  │      is seen)                 │  ║ ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║  └──────────────────────────────┘  ║ ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ╚════════════════════════════════════╝ ║ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ╚══════════════════════════════════════════╝ ║ ║      │\n   │   ║   ╚════════════════════════════════════════════════╝ ║      │\n   │   ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝      │\n   │                                                                │\n   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n   Each space contains the previous as its substrate.\n   The yogi penetrates from #1 → #5; each transition is a deepening.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.4  The Nine Cakras","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────  brahma-randhra (crown)\n                         ▲\n                         │\n                   ┌─────┴─────┐\n                   │ 9. soma   │  ◄── moon-cakra (sahasrāra region)\n                   │   -cakra  │\n                   └─────┬─────┘\n                         │\n                   ┌─────┴─────┐\n                   │ 8. manas- │  ◄── above ājñā\n                   │   cakra   │\n                   └─────┬─────┘\n                         │\n                   ┌─────┴─────┐\n                   │ 7. ājñā   │  ◄── between brows  (nīla-jyotis)\n                   └─────┬─────┘\n                         │\n                   ┌─────┴─────┐\n                   │ 6. lalanā │  ◄── root of soft palate / uvular zone\n                   └─────┬─────┘                    (M-B addition)\n                         │\n                   ┌─────┴─────┐\n                   │ 5. viśuddha│  ◄── throat\n                   └─────┬─────┘\n                         │\n                   ┌─────┴─────┐\n                   │ 4. anāhata│  ◄── heart  (antar-lakṣya, B2.2)\n                   └─────┬─────┘\n                         │\n                   ┌─────┴─────┐\n                   │ 3. maṇi-  │  ◄── navel\n                   │   pūra    │\n                   └─────┬─────┘\n                         │\n                   ┌─────┴─────┐\n                   │ 2. svādhi-│  ◄── genital region\n                   │   ṣṭhāna  │\n                   └─────┬─────┘\n                         │\n                   ┌─────┴─────┐\n                   │ 1. mūlā-  │  ◄── perineum  (kuṇḍalinī seat)\n                   │   dhāra   │\n                   └───────────┘\n\n   The Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa adds three stations to the standard six:\n   lalanā (between viśuddha and ājñā), manas-cakra (above ājñā),\n   soma-cakra (in the sahasrāra zone).</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.5  Five Doṣas vs. Five Lakṣaṇas","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n   ┌─────────────────────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n   │  FIVE DOṢAS  (obstacles)         │ │  FIVE LAKṢAṆAS  (signs)         │\n   │  (B4.1)                          │ │  (B4.2)                          │\n   ├─────────────────────────────────┤ ├─────────────────────────────────┤\n   │  1. kāma          (desire)       │ │  1. anāmayatva   (free of illness)│\n   │  2. krodha        (anger)        │ │  2. alolupatva   (no craving)   │\n   │  3. niḥśvāsa      (irreg. breath)│ │  3. indriya-jaya (sense-mastery)│\n   │  4. bhaya         (fear)         │ │  4. dṛḍha-āsana  (firm posture) │\n   │  5. nidrā-ālasya  (sleep+lethargy)│ │ 5. dhyāna-sthairya (steady    │\n   │                                  │ │                     contemplation)│\n   └─────────────────────────────────┘ └─────────────────────────────────┘\n            obstacles dissolved              accomplishments arisen\n                  ↓                                  ↓\n            yoga-abhyāsena                    siddhasya yoginaḥ\n              nivartante                         jñeyāḥ</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.6  Path-Map","title":"Brāhmaṇa by Brāhmaṇa","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n   B1.1   ──── Yājñavalkya ascends to āditya-maṇḍala\n   B1.2   ──── 15-limb yoga: limbs 1-5  (yama … deśa)\n   B1.3   ──── 15-limb yoga: limbs 6-10 (kāla … dṛk-sthiti)\n   B1.4   ──── 15-limb yoga: limbs 11-15 (prāṇa-saṃyama … samādhi)\n   B1.5   ──── three lakṣyas introduced\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   B2.1   ──── tāraka twofold: mūrti / a-mūrti = amanaska\n   B2.2   ──── antar-lakṣya: heart-lotus, koṭi-sūrya, mano-laya\n   B2.3   ──── bahir-lakṣya: 5 colour-spaces, vaiṣṇavaṃ padam\n   B2.4   ──── madhya-lakṣya: 5 ākāśas introduced\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   B3.1   ──── 5 ākāśas enumerated\n   B3.2   ──── ākāśa — bare inner dark\n   B3.3   ──── parākāśa, mahākāśa\n   B3.4   ──── sūryākāśa  →  videha-mukti\n   B3.5   ──── paramākāśa →  kaivalya\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   B4.1   ──── 5 doṣas\n   B4.2   ──── 5 lakṣaṇas\n   B4.3   ──── 9 cakras\n   B4.4   ──── jīvanmukta defined (7 attributes)\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   B5.1   ──── videha-mukti precisely defined\n   B5.2   ──── the supreme state (anubhūti-mātra-sāra)\n   B5.3   ──── phala-śruti — punar-āvṛttiḥ na vidyate</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Part V · Practical Steps & Observations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>On reading this part — The Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa is structured as a complete practice manual; what follows is the sequence it itself prescribes, rendered into a working protocol. Italicised passages are observations taken from the text or Brahma-Yogin’s Dīpikā; non-italicised passages are derivations a reader can apply.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"V.1  The Five Doṣas as Diagnostic","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Before practice, check honestly against the five doṣas (B4.1). Where any is currently strong, the deeper limbs will not yield.","<table><tr><td>Doṣa</td><td>How it shows up</td><td>Antidote within the 15-limb scheme</td></tr><tr><td>kāma (desire)</td><td>The mind keeps returning to a desired object during sitting.</td><td>yama (esp. brahma-carya) + tyāga.</td></tr><tr><td>krodha (anger)</td><td>Residual irritation from the day disturbs the sitting.</td><td>yama (ahiṃsā) + mauna.</td></tr><tr><td>niḥśvāsa (irregular breath)</td><td>Sighing, holding, or shallow breath during sitting.</td><td>prāṇa-saṃyama (limb 11).</td></tr><tr><td>bhaya (fear)</td><td>Restlessness without a named object; resistance to deepening.</td><td>ātma-dhyāna (limb 14) — fear has no purchase on the recognised Self.</td></tr><tr><td>nidrā-ālasya (sleep + lethargy)</td><td>Drowsy sitting; reluctance to begin.</td><td>kāla (limb 6: brāhma-muhūrta) + āsana (limb 7) + deha-sāmya (limb 9).</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"V.2  The 15-Limb Yoga as Operating Sequence","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The limbs are intended sequentially, not as parallel options. The Upaniṣad’s order is itself the protocol.","Establish the moral ground: yama, niyama, tyāga, mauna, deśa.","Establish the postural ground: kāla (when), āsana (how), mūla-bandha (energetic seal), deha-sāmya (symmetry), dṛk-sthiti (steady gaze).","Establish the inner ground: prāṇa-saṃyama, pratyāhāra, dhāraṇā.","Move to ātma-dhyāna — and only ātma-dhyāna. Object-meditation belongs to earlier practice.","Allow samādhi. It is not a sixteenth thing to do; it is the natural outcome when the prior fifteen are stable."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"V.3  A Single Sitting (60 min)","title":"Drawing Together the Three Lakṣyas","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Phase</td><td>Duration</td><td>What you do</td><td>What the Upaniṣad says you watch for</td></tr><tr><td>Preparation</td><td>5 min</td><td>Settle into deha-sāmya, dṛk-sthiti; three slow breaths.</td><td>Body symmetrical; gaze steady forward; doṣas named.</td></tr><tr><td>Antar-lakṣya (B2.2)</td><td>15 min</td><td>Eyes closed. Contemplate the eight-petalled heart-lotus and the koṭi-sūrya radiance within.</td><td>Criterion: mano līyate — the mind dissolves into the light, does not picture it.</td></tr><tr><td>Bahir-lakṣya (B2.3)</td><td>10 min</td><td>Eyes open, soft forward gaze. Allow the colour-spaces (blue, dark, red, yellow, white, sky-coloured) to appear.</td><td>The light becomes anudṛṣṭa (continuously perceived), not flashing.</td></tr><tr><td>Madhya-lakṣya (B2.4 / B3.2-5)</td><td>15 min</td><td>Half-open eyes. Move through the five ākāśas — ākāśa, parākāśa, mahākāśa, sūryākāśa, paramākāśa.</td><td>Each is recognised as the substrate of the previous; never as a separate object.</td></tr><tr><td>Śāmbhavī (cross-reference Advaya-tāraka m.14)</td><td>10 min</td><td>Outer gaze fixed; inner attention on the inner light; no blink.</td><td>Both stances held without strain.</td></tr><tr><td>Settling</td><td>5 min</td><td>Drop all method. Sit.</td><td>Whatever remains; do not name it.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"V.4  Reading the Text as Practice","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The phala-śruti (B5.3) names a specific act — nityam adhīte (daily study). This is not the same as “daily chanting.” The Brahma-Yogin reads it as study with comprehension. A practical reading schedule:","Day 1: Brāhmaṇa I (the 15 limbs). Mark the limbs you do and do not address.","Day 2: Brāhmaṇa II (the three lakṣyas elaborated). Map them to your current sitting.","Day 3: Brāhmaṇa III (the five ākāśas). Try the discriminations at prātaḥ-kāla.","Day 4: Brāhmaṇa IV (doṣas, lakṣaṇas, 9 cakras, jīvanmukta). Run the diagnostics.","Day 5: Brāhmaṇa V (videhamukti, supreme state, phala-śruti). Sit with mantra B5.2 until it settles.","Day 6–7: Reread the whole text in one sitting. Repeat the cycle.","Over a year this yields ~50 cycles — the depth the Upaniṣad means by nityam adhīte."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"V.5  Observations the Text Implicitly Makes","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Object-meditation has a ceiling. The 14th limb is specifically ātma-dhyāna; meditation on anything other than the Self does not lead to samādhi here, even if it produces other yogic effects. (B1.4.)","The three lakṣyas are stances of the antaḥkaraṇa, not three different objects. Treating them as three places looked at is to remain at the surface. (B1.5, cross-ref Advaya-tāraka mantra 4.)","The five ākāśas nest. Each is the substrate of the previous, not a parallel inner room. (B2.4, B3.x.)","Videhamukti is not a different attainment from jīvanmukti. The same realisation underlies both; only the bodily condition differs. (B4.4 ↔ B5.1.)","Sūryākāśa corresponds to videhamukti, paramākāśa to kaivalya. The text matches each ākāśa to a specific liberation-state. (B3.4–B3.5.)","The nine cakras are subtler stations than the standard six, used after the six are stabilised. They are not optional refinements; they are part of the same suṣumnā axis. (B4.3.)","The yoga is fifteen limbs, not eight. Anyone teaching this Upaniṣad by mapping it onto Patañjali’s eight has already cut off half its content. (B1.2–B1.4.)"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"V.6  Common Errors the Text Implicitly Names","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Skipping the moral preparation. Practitioners who begin at prāṇa-saṃyama or pratyāhāra (limbs 11–12) without yama-niyama-tyāga (limbs 1–3) build on sand.","Treating sit-time as meditation. Mauna (limb 4) and deśa (limb 5) are separate limbs precisely because where and how you sit matters as much as that you sit.","Object-meditation as the final practice. Limb 14 is ātma-dhyāna. Continuing with object-meditation indefinitely is mistaking the 13th limb (dhāraṇā) for the goal.","Counting ākāśas as places. They are depths. Treating them as five visual rooms is to remain in citra-ākāśa indefinitely.","Pursuing siddhis as confirmation. The lakṣaṇas of B4.2 are functional, not paranormal. If you require paranormal confirmation, you are still in the doṣa of kāma.","Mistaking inner light for the Self. The lights are seen; the Self is the seer. Brāhmaṇa V is unsparing: tatra na kaścil lakṣyo na draṣṭā na darśanam.","Daily recitation without daily study. The phala-śruti names nityam adhīte (study), not mere recitation. Mechanical chanting yields none of the promised fruits."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Part VI · References","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Primary text — Sanskrit","Yoga Upaniṣad-s, with the commentary of Śrī Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin. Adyar Library Sanskrit edition (Adyar: Adyar Library and Research Centre, 1920; subsequent reprintings). The standard Sanskrit text of all 20 Yoga Upaniṣads with the only classical Sanskrit commentary on each.","108 Upaniṣads. Anandāśrama Sanskrit Series (Pune) and Nirṇaya-Sāgara editions (Bombay) carry the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa in slightly varying recensions.","Yoga-Upaniṣad-saṃgraha. Adyar critical reissue, useful for comparative readings across the cognate Yoga Upaniṣads.","Translations and modern editions","Ayyaṅgār, T. R. Śrīnivāsa &amp; Murti, G. Srinivasa (tr.). The Yoga Upaniṣad-s. Adyar: Adyar Library, 1938 (later reprintings). The standard English translation; follows Brahma-Yogin’s Dīpikā throughout.","Aiyar, K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi (tr.). Thirty Minor Upanishads. Madras: Vasanta Press / Theosophical Publishing House, 1914. Includes the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa among the thirty.","Deussen, Paul. Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda. Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus, 1897. English: Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda, tr. V. M. Bedekar &amp; G. B. Palsule. Delhi: Motilal Banarsidass, 1980. Includes Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa in the Yoga-Upaniṣad section.","Warrier, A. G. Krishna (tr.). The Yoga Upaniṣads. Madras: Theosophical Publishing House / Adyar Library, 1989. Modern English revision of the 1914 and 1938 work.","Expositional traditions cited","Swami Sivananda. The Yoga Upanishads. Rishikesh: Divine Life Society. Practical / sādhana-focused readings; especially attentive to the lakṣya and cakra material.","Swami Krishnananda. Studies in Upaniṣadic Philosophy and Selected lectures. Rishikesh: Divine Life Society. Philosophical / Advaita-Vedānta readings of the Yoga Upaniṣads.","Cognate texts referenced in this companion","Advaya-tāraka Upaniṣad — the closest cognate; same three lakṣyas, more compact treatment; pair the two for full understanding.","Tejo-bindu Upaniṣad — closest in tone to Brāhmaṇa V; advaitic culmination.","Yoga-kuṇḍalinī Upaniṣad — fuller treatment of suṣumnā and the cakras invoked in B4.3.","Yoga-cūḍāmaṇi Upaniṣad — alternate witness to the nine-cakra scheme.","Haṭha-yoga-pradīpikā of Svātmārāma — chapter 4 cross-references the śāmbhavī and amanaska material implicit in B2.1.","Yoga-sūtra of Patañjali with Vyāsa-bhāṣya — for comparison with the fifteen-limb yoga (cf. IV.1 diagram).","Maṇḍūkya Upaniṣad with Gauḍapāda-kārikā — for the advaita-frame presupposed by Brāhmaṇas IV–V.","Secondary scholarship","Larson, Gerald J. &amp; Bhattacharya, Ram Shankar (eds.). Yoga: India’s Philosophy of Meditation. Encyclopedia of Indian Philosophies, vol. XII. Delhi: Motilal Banarsidass, 2008. — Survey volume; sections on the Yoga Upaniṣads situate the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa historically.","Mallinson, James &amp; Singleton, Mark. Roots of Yoga. London: Penguin Classics, 2017. — Modern critical reader of yoga sources; translations of select Yoga-Upaniṣad passages with critical apparatus.","Bouy, Christian. Les Nātha-Yogin et les Upaniṣads. Paris: De Boccard, 1994. — Detailed study of the relationship between the Yoga Upaniṣads (including the Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa) and the Nātha lineage.","Birch, Jason. \"The Amanaska: King of All Yogas. A Critical Edition and Annotated Translation.\" Doctoral dissertation, University of Oxford, 2013. — Essential for the wider amanaska-yoga corpus invoked in B2.1.","Vidyāraṇya. Jīvanmukti-viveka. (Multiple editions, e.g., Adyar 1978.) — Classical systematisation of the jīvanmukti/videhamukti distinction anticipated in B4.4–B5.1."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Closing Note","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This companion presents the entire Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa Upaniṣad as five brāhmaṇas (twenty-one passages plus the śānti-pāṭha) in Devanāgarī and IAST with literal pada-by-pada gloss, English translation, and a five-commentary digest per passage. Brahma-Yogin’s Dīpikā is the only classical Sanskrit commentary; the other four commentary voices (Ayyangar, Sivananda, Krishnananda, Deussen) are the canonical modern expositional traditions of this document series.","What this companion does NOT do: reproduce Brahma-Yogin’s Dīpikā verbatim. The Adyar Sanskrit edition remains indispensable for that. The commentary digests here are in English, drawn from the Dīpikā’s structure and each tradition’s characteristic readings; they are a working synthesis, not a verbatim translation.","Pair this document with: the Sanskrit text (Adyar); Ayyangar’s English (Adyar 1938); the cognate Advaya-tāraka companion (in this same folder) for the compact statement of the three-lakṣya doctrine; and, where possible, sustained work with a teacher. The Upaniṣad opens with Yājñavalkya approaching Āditya (B1.1) — the transmission is by relationship, not by reading alone."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
